# .gitignore proposal for the public reproducibility repository
# (swift-java-microservices, IITSOE 2026 revision)
#
# Principle: the repository must contain everything needed to re-derive every
# number in the paper, and nothing that is regenerated by a build or that is
# too large to survive a clone. Where a rule would hide data a reviewer needs,
# the rule carries an explicit negation instead of being widened.
#
# Two things this file cannot express, and which are therefore enforced by
# sync_to_public.sh instead:
#   1. a file size ceiling (git ignore patterns match names, not sizes)
#   2. refusing to copy nested .git directories out of vendored checkouts

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# macOS and editor noise
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride
._*
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Swift build output
#
# services/swift/*/.build/ is ~43,000 files on the working copy, including
# vendored SwiftPM checkouts that carry their own .git directories. Copying
# those into this repository would register them as embedded repositories and
# break a plain clone.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
.build/
.swiftpm/
DerivedData/
*.xcodeproj/
*.xcworkspace/

# Package.resolved is deliberately NOT ignored.
# It pins the exact Vapor, Fluent, SwiftNIO and postgres-nio versions the
# published numbers were produced with. For a reproducibility artifact this is
# the single most load-bearing lockfile in the repository; ignoring it (as the
# current published .gitignore does) means a reviewer who clones and builds
# resolves to whatever is newest and cannot reproduce the build.
!services/swift/*/Package.resolved

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Java build output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
target/
*.class
*.jar
*.war
*.ear
.mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.jar
!**/src/main/**/target/
!**/src/test/**/target/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Python and Node tooling
# The analysis scripts are stdlib plus numpy/scipy; no vendored packages.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/
.ipynb_checkpoints/
node_modules/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# IDE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
.idea/
.vscode/
*.iml
*.iws

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets and local environment
#
# infrastructure/.env holds the local Postgres password and the Grafana admin
# credentials. They are throwaway local values, but a public repository is the
# wrong place to normalise committing a file called .env. The committed
# .env.example carries the same keys with placeholder values.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.local
infrastructure/.env
*.secret
*.pem
*.key
!.env.example
!infrastructure/.env.example

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# k6 raw per-iteration streams (the size rule)
#
# Size ceiling nominated: 5 MB. Nothing above it is published. Justification
# from the working copy: every artifact any report or table depends on is at
# most 2.24 MB (the largest k6 console log), while the per-iteration streams
# that nothing depends on reach 388 MB and total 1.8 GB. No file in the tree
# sits between 2.24 MB and 20.3 MB, so 5 MB is a boundary no real file is near.
#
# The ceiling is a backstop, not the main mechanism. The name rule below does
# the separating, because several aborted runs produced streams of only a few
# hundred bytes and a size test alone would let those through.
#
# The old suite wrote these as <timestamp>_<scenario>-test.json, which is the
# name rule below. The revision-3 driver (benchmarking/rev3/run_suite.py) uses
# k6 --summary-export only and never writes a per-iteration stream, so rev3
# raw data is committed in full.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules below are ordered: last match wins in git. The scratch campaign is
# dropped first, the curated final/ set is re-admitted, and the per-iteration
# streams are then excluded again so that re-admitting final/ cannot drag
# 1.08 GB of stream files back in.
#
# Superseded April 2026 single-run data
#
# benchmarking/results/ (outside final/) is the scratch area of the original
# single-run campaign: repeated aborted load tests, per-run reports that were
# never cited, and 1.8 GB of streams. Reviewers criticised exactly this data.
# It is not deleted from the working copy, it is simply not published.
# benchmarking/results/final/ IS published, under an explicit SUPERSEDED
# marker, because the accepted version of the paper quotes it.
benchmarking/results/20*
benchmarking/results/reports/
benchmarking/results/*.csv
benchmarking/results/*.png
benchmarking/results/*.html

!benchmarking/results/final/
!benchmarking/results/final/**

benchmarking/results/**/*-test.json
benchmarking/rev3/results/**/*-test.json

# Note on what is deliberately NOT negated here: the *_summary.json files that
# sit loose in benchmarking/results/ (six of them, from aborted or pilot runs
# in April) stay excluded. The published boundary is the final/ directory, not
# "every small file from the old campaign". Re-admitting stray pilot summaries
# would put runs in the repository that no table in any version of the paper
# refers to, which is the opposite of what the open-data statement promises.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Logs
#
# Blanket *.log, then re-admit the two log sets that are evidence: the curated
# April final-suite console output already published, and the rev3 per-run k6
# logs, which record the exact stage schedule and threshold outcome per run.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
*.log
logs/
!benchmarking/results/final/*.log
!benchmarking/rev3/results/**/*.log

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Revision-3 working state
#
# rev3 results are the evidence for the revised paper and are committed.
# Excluded: the resumable driver's partial files and any k6 export that was
# interrupted mid-write (run_suite.py writes <run>.json.tmp then renames).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
benchmarking/rev3/results/**/*.tmp
benchmarking/rev3/results/**/*.partial
benchmarking/rev3/**/.ipynb_checkpoints/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Runtime data from the observability stack
# Grafana writes its SQLite database and session state here at run time.
# The provisioned dashboards and datasources under monitoring/grafana/
# are committed; only the data directory is not.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
monitoring/grafana/data/
monitoring/prometheus/data/
monitoring/loki/data/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Archives and captures
# Nothing in this project legitimately produces these; if one appears it is a
# hand-made bundle that should not enter history.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
*.tar
*.tar.gz
*.tgz
*.zip
*.pcap

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Author working material that must not reach the public repository
#
# The manuscript, the reviewer correspondence, the similarity and AI-detection
# reports, and the supervision logbook live outside this tree today. These
# rules exist so that an accidental copy into the repository is not committed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
docs/jadwal-riset.md
docs/logbook-template.md
docs/research-proposal.md
*.docx
*.xlsx
revision-work/
**/Turnitin*
**/similarity*

# Runs measured before the aggregation window was corrected. Warm-up requests
# and the health probe were reaching the service histograms without reaching
# the client metrics, which pulled the server-side percentiles down. These runs
# were repeated and are kept locally only as a record of the correction.
# Publishing them would put superseded numbers next to the ones the paper
# reports, which is the exact confusion this release exists to remove.
benchmarking/rev3/results_discarded_prewarmupfix/
